# t0942 — the `unit` / `*mut unit` REPRESENTATIONS of a Callable. `.clone()` on
# these cannot resolve a dispatch name, and NO `GirType::FnPtr` arm can reach
# them, so t0936's fix (which heals the FnPtr cells) leaves these RED by
# construction.
#
#   Callable[int(int)] cb       PARAMETER        -> GIR `unit`
#   Callable[int(int)] &cb      PARAMETER        -> GIR `*mut unit`
#   int(int) f = dbl            bare fn-type     -> GIR `unit`
#
# src/ir/lowering/types.rs:190-205 states all three and gives the verification
# command (`gg build --emit-gir`). ALL THREE are exercised below — a fixture's
# name is a claim about scope (Core #12), and this one claims all three.
#
# ⛔ DO NOT "FIX" THIS WITH A `GirType::Unit` ARM in the dispatch-name ladder:
# that routes EVERY unit-typed receiver through the closure clone path and is a
# strictly worse miscompile than the bug. The representation must carry the
# type, which is an upstream (erasure) fix.
#
# Before R48 Track R each cell was a silent rc-139 SEGV. Since that round's
# Core #10 lower-or-reject guard at the method-dispatch gate they are a LOUD
# lowering rejection instead — still wrong, but no longer memory-unsafe.
#
# ggdef adjudicates the param and `&`-param cells correctly (prints 2 for each).
# INTENDED: prints 2 three times.
int dbl_plus(int x):
    return x + 1

int apply_it(Callable[int(int)] cb):
    Callable[int(int)] g = cb.clone()
    return g(1)

int apply_ref(Callable[int(int)] &cb):
    Callable[int(int)] g = cb.clone()
    return g(1)

void main():
    # cell 1 — `Callable[..]` PARAMETER, GIR `unit`
    print(apply_it((int x): x + 1))

    # cell 2 — `Callable[..] &` PARAMETER, GIR `*mut unit`
    Callable[int(int)] h = (int x): x + 1
    print(apply_ref(&h))

    # cell 3 — bare fn-type spelling, GIR `unit`
    int(int) f = dbl_plus
    int(int) g = f.clone()
    print(g(1))
